## 5. PIECE 4 — NOTIFICATIONS (reminders + the safety re-check)

The document needs: automated re-test reminders, scheduled review reminders, and
the recurring symptom safety check at every follow-up.

**Key decision for the non-coder — two types, use the simpler one where you can:**
- **Local notifications** (scheduled on the device) — for things the app already knows the date of: "your re-test is due", "time for your review", "monthly check-in". No server needed. Use `expo-notifications` scheduling. **Prefer this** for the document's re-test/review reminders.
- **Remote push** (server-triggered) — only needed for things triggered from outside the device (e.g. "your results are ready", "clinician approved your plan"). This needs the Expo push service + stored push tokens + an Edge Function to send.

Both require a **development build via EAS Build — push does NOT work in Expo Go.**
Flag this to the founder clearly: Phase 4 is where you move from Expo Go testing
to an EAS development/production build. That's a one-time setup, and it's also
required before you can ship to the App Store / Play Store anyway.

Install:
```bash
npx expo install expo-notifications expo-device expo-constants
```

Build:
- A `registerForPushNotifications()` helper that asks permission, gets the `ExpoPushToken` (using the EAS `projectId`), and saves it to `push_tokens`. Handle permission-denied gracefully — the app must work fine without notifications.
- **Local scheduling** for re-test and review reminders, created when a follow-up row is written (reuse Phase 2's `follow_ups`).
- **The recurring symptom safety check:** on a set cadence, a notification brings the user back to the Phase 1 `SymptomInterrupt` — any "yes" still routes to Pathway B. This is the document's "re-triage at every follow-up," delivered.
- One send-push Edge Function for the outside-triggered events (results ready, plan approved), reading tokens from `push_tokens`. Respect Expo's 600-notifications/sec limit — batch if needed.

